e News", "source_priority": 14, "source_url": "https://news.google.com", "source_icon": "https://i.newsdata.io/google.png", "sentiment": "negative", "sentiment_stats": {"negative": 88.55, "neutral": 10.47, "positive": 0.98}, "ai_tag": ["artificial intelligence"], "ai_region": "ONLY AVAILABLE IN CORPORATE PLANS", "ai_org": "ONLY AVAILABLE IN CORPORATE PLANS", "ai_summary": "ONLY AVAILABLE IN PAID PLANS", "duplicate": false}, {"article_id": "9b6979e9064d4bf9aa5db3beac4154b2", "link": "https://www.pcworld.com/article/3292923/microsoft-fallout-nvidia-security-questions-and-other-must-read-stories-from-pcworld-sister-sites.html", "title": "Microsoft fallout, Nvidia security questions and other must-read stories from PCWorld sister sites", "description": "Well, that was a rough week for big tech. Microsoft’s global M365 meltdown ended about a week ago today, but it was followed by system hiccups at four U.S. banks. Then glitches in United Airlines systems grounded flights on Monday, followed by Apple systems crashing on Tuesday. It’s enough to make one turn to journalists for some follow-up analysis. So, as we tour the week’s most interesting stories from Foundry tech sites, I want to stop first at CIO’s “Microsoft keeps failing. Why can’t its customers leave?” by contributor Ryan Triplette. The basic answer: “Customer captivity is the problem,” she writes. “Customers have been steered, by design, into buying everything from one vendor with no practical way out.” Ironically, this draft is being written in Microsoft Word, and, yes, I do feel trapped. Meanwhile, over at Computerworld, Nidhi Singal reports on how hackers exploited the M365 outage to launch phishing campaigns. Because of course they did. Read this article, savvy tech user, and consider it a reminder to tell your less savvy friends and family about ways to evade phishing scams and social engineering. We need to talk about AI (again) CIO had another intriguing op-ed in Nazar Yaremchuk’s analysis of how AI-generated software development often just shifts workloads downstream. His article focuses on software-dev, but CIO has written before on the “invisible tax” of “AI workslop” in other parts of organizations. With a more AI-positive perspective, let’s turn to PCWorld’s own Brad Chacos, executive editor, who writes about how Nvidia’s RTX Spark Mini PC became the enabling hardware for a massive personal research project. If you’re into (legal) sports betting and interested in the liberating possibilities of local AI, give it a read. Brad also interviewed semiconductor expert Dr. Ian Cutress to help explain, “Why Nvidia can’t simply make more GeForce cards — even if it wants to.” Let’s stick with Nvidia, and spend some time with this TechCrunch story by editor-in-chief Connie Loizos, who asks, “Where was Nvidia’s security team?” This is a must-read for anyone who’s been wondering if Jensen Huang’s reported affair with a suspected spy ever raised concerns inside Nvidia’s CSO office. Gadgets, gadgets, and more gadgets Okay, more AI — and I know I’m ping-ponging between “AI good, AI bad,” but just bear with me. TechCrunch doesn’t do a lot of product coverage, but this tour of “six AI-powered gadgets that make your home office smarter” by Lauren Forristal is a timely read before Black Friday sales. You should also check out two articles from Macworld. One of the team’s German region editors, Halyna Kubiv, conducted an excellent deep dive on the Apple Watch’s heart rate accuracy. (Short answer: It’s very accurate if we believe a Polar H10 chest monitor is a reliable baseline.) Finally, one of Macworld’s UK editors, David Price, reported on the troubled launch of Apple’s HomeView smart hub. Price shares various public message board posts, including this gem: “The hardware itself is good, but in my opinion, the HomeView is the WORST Apple launch ever and should have been delayed further.” Sounds more like a Microsoft user than an Apple user, right?", "content": "ONLY AVAILABLE IN P